  "title": "LeetCode 149: Max Points in Line",
  "date": "2026-11-22T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "LeetCode 149: Max Points in Line\n\nRecently, I started going through LeetCode in several languages to find interesting aspects of each. Here is the link.\n\nGiven an array points of unique 2D points where points\\[i] = \\[xi, yi], return the maximum number of points that lie on the same straight line.\n\nInput: points = \\[\\[1,1],\\[2,2],\\[3,3]]\n\nOutput: 3\n\nInput: points = \\[\\[1,1],\\[3,2],\\[5,3],\\[4,1],\\[2,3],\\[1,4]]\n\nOutput: 4\n\nConstraints:\n\n1 <= points.length <= 300\n\npoints\\[i].length == 2\n\n-10^4 <= xi, yi <= 10^4\n\nall points are unique.\n\nThis is not a hard question. You can fix one point $(x\\_i, y\\_i)$, then find another one $(x\\_j, y\\_j)$ and calculate the slope $dy/dx$. Count each slope; the slope with the highest count means the most points lie on the same line.\n\nThe hard part for me is that I am not clear about the constant numbers in each language, or about float calculations in compiled languages.\n\nInfinity\n\n|language| positive infinity |\n|--------|-------------------|\n| TypeScript| Infinity               |\n| C++ | numeric\\_limits::infinity() |\n| Java | Float.POSITIVE\\_INFINITY |\n| Python | float('inf') |\n| C# | Double.PositiveInfinity |\n| Go | Math.Inf(1) |\n| Kotlin | Double.POSITIVE\\_INFINITY |\n| Swift | Double.infinity |\n| Rust | f64::INFINITY |\n\nHandling Double Sign\n\nFor the Double type in many languages, 0.0 and -0.0 are two distinct values. In this case, when using the slope as a key, you need to normalize the sign by adding 0.0.\n\nHashMap Usage\n\nFor TypeScript\n\nFor C++\n\nFor Java\n\nFor Python\n\nFor C#\n\nFor Go\n\nFor Kotlin\n\nFor Swift\n\nFor Rust\n\nWhat is more\n\nSwift's if grammar is so elegant, but remember to add a space between the exclamation mark and the variable, because j! means force-unwrapping j.\n\nRust has very strict limitations on strings. In other languages you can usually treat a string as an array, but in Rust you should use s.as\\_bytes(), and the indexes are usize. This is even more noticeable in Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters.",
